================================================================================
ALGORITHMS AND RESEARCH
================================================================================

These are published methods reimplemented from their descriptions. No code was
copied from the sources below; the citations are intellectual credit.

Glicko-2 rating system
    Mark E. Glickman. "Example of the Glicko-2 system" (2013) and the
    Glicko-2 specification. http://www.glicko.net/glicko.html
    Reimplemented in app_logic/lib/src/glicko2.dart.

FSRS (Free Spaced Repetition Scheduler), version 4
    Open Spaced Repetition. https://github.com/open-spaced-repetition
    The reference implementation is MIT. Chess Gym contains an independent
    pure-Dart reimplementation in app_logic/lib/src/fsrs.dart; default weights
    are the published FSRS-4 defaults.

Chess aesthetics / interestingness scoring
    Azlan Iqbal et al., including "A Discrete Computational Aesthetics Model
    for a Zero-sum Perfect Information Game" and related AAAI work.
    Informs the interestingness formula in pipeline/stages/score.py.
    Research influence only; no code derived.

Desirable difficulty
    Robert A. Bjork. Informs the upward-biased rating band in
    app_logic/lib/src/selection.dart.

Chunking and chess expertise
    William G. Chase and Herbert A. Simon, "Perception in Chess" (1973).
    Adriaan de Groot, "Thought and Choice in Chess" (1946).
    Background for the theme taxonomy and progression design.


